{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>Now Laban had gone to shear his sheep.</b> For the flock was in the hands of his sons a three days' journey away, as it is written, Therefore it was told to Laban on the third day." "<b>The terafim.</b> Some say it is a bronze instrument made to know the divisions of the hours, and others said that there is power in the masters of the constellations to make a figure at known times and the figure will speak, and their proof is \"for the terafim speak worthlessness\" (Zechariah 10:2), but the meaning of the verse is not so. The nearer to me is that the terafim are in the shape of human beings and it is made to receive the power of supernal beings, and I cannot explain. The proof that the terafim are so: the terafim that Michal daughter of Saul placed in the bed, so that the keepers of the house thought it was David. And the verse that says \"no ephod and terafim\" (Hosea 3:4) has two interpretations. The first: he said there is no king and no prince in Israel, for HaShem chose a king only from the family of David; therefore he said in the second chapter \"and they shall seek HaShem their Elohim and David their king\" (ibid. v. 5), and there is no sacrifice and no pillar to idolatry, and there is no ephod that the worshippers of Baal would make in the likeness of the ephod of Moses; therefore he said ephod and did not say the ephod, as in \"Bring the ephod\" (1 Samuel 23:9), \"an ephod went down in his hand\" (ibid. 14:3 [ sic ]) — it is not the ephod that Moses made, and in its place I will explain it with complete proofs, if HaShem wills. And the second interpretation: they will neither worship HaShem nor idolatry. Now, Laban her father called the terafim his elohim. Some say that Rachel stole them to remove idolatry from her father, but if this were so, why did she take them with her and not hide them on the road? The nearer is that Laban her father knew constellations, and she feared that her father would look at the constellations to know by which road they fled."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:46:40.166474154Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}